  <section class="wh-fit" aria-labelledby="wh-fit-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="eyebrow">Fitment check</div>
      <h2 id="wh-fit-h">What we check before a wheel goes on</h2>
      <p class="wh-lead">A wheel that looks right can still rub, miss the brakes or sit crooked. Here is what our team looks at. This is a general guide, not a guarantee for any specific wheel.</p>
      <div class="wh-fit-grid">
        <div><b>1 · Bolt pattern</b><p>The number of lugs and the circle they sit on, written like 5x114.3. It has to match the vehicle exactly.</p></div>
        <div><b>2 · Offset</b><p>How far the wheel face sits from the centerline, in millimeters. It sets how far the wheel and tire stick out or tuck in.</p></div>
        <div><b>3 · Clearance</b><p>Room for brake calipers, suspension parts and fenders at full turn and full suspension travel, especially with wider tires or a lift.</p></div>
        <div><b>4 · Center bore and load</b><p>The center hole should fit the hub, and the wheel and tire should be rated for the vehicle's weight.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="wh-guar"><span class="sample-note">Fitment guarantee terms: Simon to confirm</span><span>No promise is made here until the shop writes the terms.</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

